{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["The checklist is transparent and low-bandwidth; expert intuition is opaque and high-bandwidth.","The panel consists of non-experts who may lack the tacit knowledge to interpret checklist items correctly.","The expert's continuous, associative representation may capture holistic patterns the discretized checklist misses."],"overturn_conditions":["If the panel cannot interpret the checklist items without expert tacit knowledge, reliability gain vanishes.","If the task is Type II (holistic, fluid), the decomposition fails and the panel underperforms the expert."],"shared_structure":["Both decompose a complex judgment into a structured procedure.","Both rely on aggregation across independent observations to cancel noise.","Both face a distinction between following the procedure and achieving the underlying goal."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["The checklist-to-expert transfer works when the task's diagnostic features are explicit and decomposable.","The compliance/reliability distinction transfers: a genuinely reliable system tracks the answer, while a compliant system merely tracks the cue."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A trained non-expert panel using an explicit checklist will exhibit higher reliability than a single expert for objectively verifiable tasks, because the checklist operationalizes the causal features the expert uses unconsciously, and aggregation cancels residual idiosyncratic noise.","counterconditions":["If the task requires holistic, non-decomposable pattern matching (Type II), the checklist will fragment the gestalt and fail.","If the expert's intuition relies on tacit knowledge not captured by the explicit checklist, the panel will be systematically biased.","If the task distribution shifts and the checklist becomes stale, panel accuracy will drop sharply while the expert adapts."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist decomposes the task into independently verifiable cues, reducing Type I processing variance and bias. Panel aggregation further cancels individual noise, producing a combined estimate closer to the objective answer than a single expert whose unaided intuition is subject to mood, fatigue, and heuristic drift.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","b16139b528b61743ec344333a53da32e58fcedde881d406121ab803fa593d9b0","c385096cc08f41a6167be8c0147e92c7abe56e68ea34a0c74bf511f14979dd08"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["e3d2d2d230e7d9dac52b0ec77389382a34dde7384fdcc78017775a23504659b1","8025e7e1acecaab5efdc524f31eb657334652532e1be7c0a3471c96d7b6f30df"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A compliant checklist is a fixed, rigid proxy; a genuinely reliable one is a dynamic, robust tracker.","The panel may achieve compliance without understanding; the expert may have understanding without explicit compliance."],"overturn_conditions":["If the evaluation demonstrates that the panel's accuracy is invariant under perturbations that change the cue-answer mapping, the mere-compliance hypothesis is overturned."],"shared_structure":["Both systems face a distinction between superficial and deep tracking of the target variable.","Both are vulnerable to Goodhart's law when the proxy becomes the target.","Both can be distinguished by stress-testing under distribution shift."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_004"],"transfer_claims":["The compliance/reliability distinction transfers: it is not enough that the panel gets the right answer on the evaluation; it must get the right answer for the right reason."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The apparent reliability gain of checklist-driven panels is often mere compliance: the panel tracks the checklist's cues, which happen to correlate with the right answer within the evaluation, but this does not constitute a genuine reliability gain because it is fragile to distribution shift and does not reflect understanding.","counterconditions":["If the checklist cues are causally necessary features of the answer, compliance and reliability coincide.","If the evaluation includes an out-of-distribution probe, a compliant panel will fail and a reliable panel will not.","If the expert's intuition also relies on the same cues, the panel offers no marginal reliability gain over the expert."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A compliant panel produces outputs that are a function of the cue structure specified by the checklist. If those cues are valid in the evaluation sample, the panel scores well. However, this is a form of overfitting to the checklist's particular cue-answer mapping. Genuine reliability requires that the panel's output be a function of the underlying answer-generating structure, which allows robustness under distribution shift.","optional_refs":["8d4f2d0d0388b96ef252ad988d4f78648b0636c42218a84533575c7000dc38cb","226bfffd3b4e0d0b3a4e3883502af89b41c45b88bb44aba85fe0ad1087c81fc4"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["e29afda9df0252e42e7b1169b9ea8a3bc3927fb425e5afb841291b4fb1099c25"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["The panel is a group of agents who could potentially notice the decoupling; a single algorithm might not.","The checklist is an external artifact the panel can override; an expert's intuition is internal and harder to override."],"overturn_conditions":["If the panel passes the cue-decoupling test by luck rather than understanding, the diagnostic is invalid.","If the expert fails the cue-decoupling test, the panel's success does not establish superiority over the expert."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a system whose output can be driven by a proxy rather than the true target.","Both can be diagnosed by decoupling the proxy from the target and observing the system's response."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_006"],"transfer_claims":["The cue-decoupling diagnostic transfers: it is a general method for distinguishing genuine from spurious reliability in any system that uses proxies."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Within a single evaluation, a genuine reliability gain is distinguished from mere checklist compliance by including adversarial items where the checklist cues are intentionally decoupled from the correct answer, and by testing whether the panel's confidence and accuracy remain calibrated under these perturbations.","counterconditions":["If the adversarial items are too far from the training distribution, even a reliable panel may fail, but its confidence should drop.","If the checklist is entirely rigid and the panel has no latitude to adjust, it cannot demonstrate reliability, only compliance.","If the expert also fails on the adversarial items, the panel's failure does not distinguish it from the expert."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A genuinely reliable panel has learned a causal model of the domain: it tracks the answer-generating structure, not just the cue. When cues are decoupled from answers, a reliable panel's confidence drops or its cue-usage shifts. A merely compliant panel follows the cue regardless, maintaining high confidence on incorrect answers. Calibration under perturbation is the within-evaluation signature of genuine reliability.","optional_refs":["226bfffd3b4e0d0b3a4e3883502af89b41c45b88bb44aba85fe0ad1087c81fc4","e29afda9df0252e42e7b1169b9ea8a3bc3927fb425e5afb841291b4fb1099c25"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["The Condorcet theorem assumes binary outcomes; checklist judgments may be multi-dimensional.","The Condorcet theorem assumes independence; panel deliberation or a shared checklist may introduce correlation."],"overturn_conditions":["If the panel's judgments are correlated due to the shared checklist, the Condorcet guarantee fails.","If the checklist does not raise individual competence above chance, aggregation does not help."],"shared_structure":["Both rely on aggregation of independent judgments to cancel noise.","Both require individual competence above chance for aggregation to work."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_003"],"transfer_claims":["The independence and competence conditions transfer: without them, aggregation does not guarantee reliability."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The Condorcet jury theorem supports the reliability of the panel over the expert only if the non-experts' judgments are independent and each has a probability of correctness greater than chance. The checklist is a mechanism for raising individual accuracy above chance, but panel deliberation may violate independence, and a checklist that is too rigid may reduce individual accuracy to mere chance on non-decomposable tasks.","counterconditions":["If the checklist items are not independent cues, the panel's aggregated judgment may be biased.","If the panel deliberates, independence is violated and the Condorcet mechanism fails.","If the task is not binary or has a high-dimensional outcome, the simple Condorcet result does not apply."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Aggregation amplifies accuracy only under the independence and competence conditions. The checklist raises competence by decomposing the task. However, if the checklist fails to capture the task's structure, individual competence drops to chance, and aggregation produces no benefit. If deliberation introduces correlation, aggregation amplifies the common error rather than canceling noise.","optional_refs":["8025e7e1acecaab5efdc524f31eb657334652532e1be7c0a3471c96d7b6f30df"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["The expert's representation is continuous and high-dimensional; the checklist is discrete and low-dimensional.","The panel is a group; the expert is an individual. The group may add social dynamics that the analogy does not capture."],"overturn_conditions":["If the expert outperforms the panel on a Type I task, the claim's prediction fails.","If the panel outperforms the expert on a Type II task, the claim's prediction fails."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a contrast between discrete, decomposable processing and continuous, holistic processing.","Both predict that the effectiveness of the processing mode depends on the task type."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_005"],"transfer_claims":["The Type I/Type II distinction transfers: it predicts when checklist-based processing will succeed and when it will fail."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"For Type I tasks (stable, decomposable feature space), the panel-plus-checklist is more reliable than the expert because the checklist enforces systematic processing of all relevant cues. For Type II tasks (fluid, holistic feature space), the expert is more reliable because the checklist cannot capture the gestalt, and panel aggregation of fragmentary cues produces a worse estimate than the expert's holistic intuition.","counterconditions":["If a Type II task can be effectively decomposed, the panel may outperform the expert.","If the expert is fatigued or biased on a Type I task, the panel's systematicity wins even if the task is decomposable.","If the boundary between Type I and Type II is fuzzy, the claim's sharp prediction may not hold."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Type I tasks have a stable, decomposable structure that can be explicitly represented in a checklist. The panel applies this structure systematically, avoiding the expert's occasional lapses. Type II tasks require holistic pattern recognition that cannot be decomposed into explicit cues. The checklist fragments the gestalt, and the panel's aggregated fragments are less accurate than the expert's holistic judgment.","optional_refs":["b16139b528b61743ec344333a53da32e58fcedde881d406121ab803fa593d9b0","c385096cc08f41a6167be8c0147e92c7abe56e68ea34a0c74bf511f14979dd08"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["SRC_008 critiques SRC_001's specific mechanism; this candidate generalizes the critique to the entire panel-vs-expert debate."],"overturn_conditions":["If the checklist is shown to eliminate systematic bias, the critique is overturned.","If a valid within-evaluation discriminator is found, the critique of the discriminator is overturned."],"shared_structure":["Both critique an underspecified causal model that relies on aggregation without addressing systematic bias.","Both identify a flaw in the proposed within-evaluation discriminator."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["The critique transfers: any claim that aggregation improves reliability must address systematic bias, not just idiosyncratic noise."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The claim that aggregation cancels idiosyncratic noise is insufficient as a causal mechanism because it does not specify how the checklist ensures that the remaining signal is the correct signal rather than a systematic bias shared by all non-experts. The within-evaluation discriminator proposed—cue decoupling—is invalid because a panel that has learned a causal model and a panel that has learned a compliant cue may both respond to cue decoupling in the same way.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is empirically validated against expert consensus and objective outcomes, systematic bias may be minimized.","If the panel includes dissenting individuals who do not share the bias, aggregation may partially cancel it.","If a better within-evaluation discriminator is proposed (e.g., causal probing of the panel's reasoning), the claim may be salvageable."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"SRC_001 asserts that aggregation cancels noise, but this is only true if the noise is idiosyncratic and uncorrelated. Non-experts using a 